        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:12:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63080,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>All sites of the peritoneal cavity (including the omentum and viscera)</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:12:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63081,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The clinical features are often nonspecific, including abdominal discomfort, symptoms of intestinal obstruction, ascites, and weight loss {19325052}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:12:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63082,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "<p>Peritoneal metastases are found in 50&ndash;80% of patients with advanced ovarian, colorectal, pancreatic, and gastric cancers {16432354; 24222942; 25891650; 28956065}. Peritoneal sarcomatosis is particularly associated with recurrence of intra-abdominal sarcomas, which represent about one&nbsp;third of all soft tissue sarcomas {30603133}. It is more common in children than in adults {25564159}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-22T09:12:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63083,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastatic cancers to the peritoneum are mainly derived from abdominopelvic organs such as the ovary, fallopian tube, uterus, colorectum, pancreas, biliary tract, and stomach {28956065; 30306591; 30503267; 30131104}, as well as from the retroperitoneum, and less commonly from the breast, lung, liver, and uterine cervix {24222942; 26457350; 28956065; 29233547; 30681378; 30589039; 23638212} or the extremities {9040804}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:12:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63084,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Peritoneum is a common site of metastasis due to its extensive area and the presence and movement of the peritoneal fluid {8849944; 17334873; 28956065}. Cancer cells can disseminate to the peritoneum by direct intraperitoneal spread in peritoneal fluid or via a lymphovascular route {17334873}. They adhere to mesothelium, permeate through mesothelial epithelium and stroma, and proliferate in a favourable tumour microenvironment {29164988; 30503267}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:12:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63085,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Peritoneal carcinomatosis is characterized by small, often superficial, whitish tumour deposits {29164988}. Peritoneal sarcomatosis forms discrete masses of variable size or diffuse thickening of the peritoneal surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:12:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63086,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>The histopathology is the same as that of the primary malignancy and includes a broad variety of carcinomas and sarcomas. Peritoneal carcinomatosis of ovarian origin is most commonly of serous type {20407318}. Among cervical cancers, the unusual tendency of gastric-type adenocarcinoma for peritoneal and omental dissemination has been noted {26457350; 29233547}. In cases with dominant mucinous morphology and scant neoplastic cellularity, the differential diagnosis of pseudomyxoma peritonei should be considered (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/34/165\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Pseudomyxoma peritonei</em>).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-10T08:03:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63087,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cytological features reflect those of the primary neoplasm. Immunohistochemistry on cell block sections may help in ascertaining the site of origin. Sarcomas show a wide variety of cytological features depending on the type {23858317}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:12:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63088,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Diagnostic molecular pathology may be useful in selected cases to determine the origin of the tumour or to assess therapeutic targets.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:12:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63089,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> cytologically malignant tumour cells; infiltrative growth may be recognizable</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> in a subset of cases, immunohistochemistry may be needed to determine the site of origin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:12:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63090,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Carcinomas and sarcomas metastatic to the peritoneum are staged according to the FIGO and Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM staging systems as appropriate to the primary origin of the tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:12:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63091,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 164,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The prognosis depends on the features of the primary cancer and may be influenced by local complications such as bowel obstruction and haemorrhage. The prognosis is poor irrespective of primary or recurrent disease and multimodal therapeutic approach {25564159; 28967566; 30603133}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:12:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]